Results. For 82 out of the 90 functions that were labeled as secure in the original datasets, we were able to construct a context in which they contain a security vulnerability.

Secure Functions (RQ1-c). 82 out of 90 secure functions contain context-dependent vulnerabilities, i.e., they would make the program vulnerable if the appropriate external context existed. In isolation, it is not possible to determine whether these functions are actually secure.
